WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.290
A suction pump can draw water ten meters up at most; that is a hard limit set not by the quality of the pump but by atmospheric pressure.

2
00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:17.220
And yet in California the topmost leaf of a redwood is drinking water one hundred sixteen meters above the ground, inside a living thing with no heart and no motor.

3
00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:24.550
The tree does not break the limit — it does not push the water, it pulls it, and stretches it like a wire about to snap.

4
00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:42.000
`podcast-science.json` bu kanalı `"language": "en"`, `kaynak_kanal: "bilim"` olarak tanımlıyor; bugünün `bilim.txt` bölümü de tam bu konu.

5
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:54.130
Sert kısıt "çeviri kaynağa sadık kalır" dediği için metni Türkçe yeniden yazmadım (o zaten `bilim.txt`) — kaynağın İngilizce çevirisini verdim; jingle ve kapanış satırlarını çıkardım.

6
00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:58.260
A suction pump can raise water about 10 meters and no higher.

7
00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:03.350
That ceiling is set not by the quality of the pump but by the weight of the atmosphere.

8
00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:13.080
And yet in California the topmost needle of a redwood is drinking water 116 meters above the ground, inside a living thing with no heart and no motor anywhere in it.

9
00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.770
The tree does not break the limit.

10
00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:22.460
It does not push the water upward — it pulls, and it holds that water stretched like a wire about to snap.

11
00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:29.630
In the Florence of the 1630s, in the gardens of the Grand Duke of Tuscany, there was a problem nobody could solve.

12
00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:39.840
The court's well-diggers had fitted a suction pump to a deep well, drawn the piston, watched the water climb the pipe, and watched it stop at a certain point for no reason anyone could give.

13
00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:43.490
They replaced the seals; nothing changed.

14
00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:46.980
They widened the pipe; nothing changed.

15
00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:52.230
They swapped the man on the handle for a stronger one; still nothing.

16
00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:59.240
The water would come up to roughly 18 braccia, about 10 meters, and then flatly refuse to rise further.

17
00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:04.330
Empty pipe above it, plenty of water below it, and nothing at all in between.

18
00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:10.220
They took the problem to the most celebrated natural philosopher of the age, the aging Galileo.

19
00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:15.470
His answer was wrong, but the way in which it was wrong was oddly ahead of its time.

20
00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:20.560
As he saw it, the rising column of water was like a rope hanging from a ceiling.

21
00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:30.770
The void had the power to pull matter, but that power was not unlimited; once the column of water passed a certain length it snapped under its own weight, exactly as a rope would.

22
00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:36.980
In other words, Galileo thought water could be pulled, but that there was a breaking point to the pulling.

23
00:02:36.980 --> 00:02:43.350
Hold on to that idea, because three hundred years later it comes back from an entirely different direction.

24
00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:48.760
The real answer arrived a few years afterward, from Galileo's student Torricelli.

25
00:02:48.760 --> 00:03:01.050
In Rome, a man named Gasparo Berti had rigged an experiment against the wall of his house with an enormous glass tube more than 10 meters tall, and had shown that the water always came to rest at the same height.

26
00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:12.860
In 1643 Torricelli ran the same experiment in miniature: he used mercury, 13.6 times denser than water, and found the mercury column standing at 76 centimeters.

27
00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:18.270
The ratio between the two numbers was precisely the ratio of the two densities.

28
00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:22.720
The meaning was plain, and it inverted the intuition of the age.

29
00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:26.050
The pump was not pulling the water up.

30
00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:35.460
The air itself, with the weight of the kilometers-thick column of atmosphere overhead, was pressing down on the surface of the water in the well and pushing it into the emptied pipe.

31
00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:45.830
At sea level that push amounted to a force of roughly 1 kilogram on every square centimeter, which is exactly enough to hold up a column of water 10.33 meters tall.

32
00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:49.640
This is not a problem engineering can solve.

33
00:03:49.640 --> 00:03:51.850
It is a piece of arithmetic.

34
00:03:51.850 --> 00:04:01.100
Build the most perfect pump on earth, draw a flawless vacuum inside the pipe, and at sea level you still cannot suck water higher than 10.33 meters,

35
00:04:01.100 --> 00:04:06.670
because the only force working in your favor is the weight of the atmosphere and that force has a hard ceiling.

36
00:04:06.670 --> 00:04:10.000
That is the physical limit of pulling from above.

37
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:14.450
If you want water to go higher, you have to push it from below.

38
00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:18.900
Now go to the coast redwood forests of northern California.

39
00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:24.630
There, in a valley whose location is kept secret, stands a tree called Hyperion.

40
00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:34.200
When it was found in 2006 it was a little over 115 meters; the measurements show it is still growing, and today it has passed 116 meters.

41
00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:37.850
Eleven times the wall that stopped the pump.

42
00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:44.380
Inside this tree there is no piston, no valve, no motor, not one contracting muscle fiber.

43
00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:47.710
Wood is not a material that moves.

44
00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:57.760
And yet on a hot summer day a large tree takes hundreds of liters of water out of the soil and carries it all the way to its crown, continuously enough to keep the needles up there wet.

45
00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:01.410
Let us clear the easy answers out of the way.

46
00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:04.100
Roots really can generate pressure.

47
00:05:04.100 --> 00:05:11.750
That is why water weeps from a maple or birch branch cut in spring, and why droplets gather on the tips of grass blades at dawn.

48
00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:17.960
But root pressure tops out around 0.1 megapascals, which at best buys you a 10-meter column.

49
00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:24.490
It largely vanishes the moment the leaves begin to transpire, and in most conifers it is barely present at all.

50
00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:27.180
Pushing from below is not enough.

51
00:05:27.180 --> 00:05:29.390
What about capillarity?

52
00:05:29.390 --> 00:05:37.680
It is true that the water-carrying pipes inside wood are extremely fine, and that in a narrow tube water will climb on its own by clinging to the walls.

53
00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:43.410
But how far it climbs is set by the diameter of the tube, and the arithmetic is merciless.

54
00:05:43.410 --> 00:05:49.940
In a typical tree conduit 20 micrometers wide, capillary rise comes to about 1.5 meters.

55
00:05:49.940 --> 00:06:02.550
If you wanted capillarity alone to lift water 100 meters, the conduits would have to be 0.15 micrometers across, and in practice no water would flow through a pipe like that at all; friction would lock everything solid.

56
00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:06.200
So the tree is neither pushing from below nor climbing.

57
00:06:06.200 --> 00:06:09.370
It is doing something else.

58
00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:14.460
To find the answer you have to go to the top and look inside a leaf.

59
00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:20.670
Cut a leaf across and you see that its interior is made of loosely packed cells with air spaces between them.

60
00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:26.400
The outer walls of those cells are a spongy, permanently damp mesh woven out of cellulose.

61
00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:30.530
On the surface of those wet walls, water meets air.

62
00:06:30.530 --> 00:06:38.980
When the pores on the underside of the leaf, the stomata, open, the outside air is drier than the air within, and water begins to evaporate.

63
00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:44.550
Every molecule that leaves drags the water behind it deeper into the pores of the cellulose mesh.

64
00:06:44.550 --> 00:06:50.120
As the water retreats, the surface at the mouth of each pore dishes inward like a tiny bowl.

65
00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:53.450
The entire mechanism is hidden in that curvature.

66
00:06:53.450 --> 00:07:00.300
The surface tension of water tries to flatten a curved surface, and in trying, it hauls the water underneath upward.

67
00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:06.990
The strength of that pull depends on how sharply the surface is bent: the narrower the pore, the stronger the pull.

68
00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:11.120
The pores in the wall of a leaf cell are on the scale of nanometers.

69
00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:16.530
In a pore with a radius of 10 nanometers the pull can reach 15 megapascals.

70
00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:21.620
For comparison, atmospheric pressure is 0.1 megapascals.

71
00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:28.790
The damp wall of a leaf in contact with dry air can therefore generate a suction a hundred times stronger than the finest vacuum pump.

72
00:07:28.790 --> 00:07:35.640
And it needs no mechanism and no expenditure of energy to do it; all it needs is dry air and a wet surface.

73
00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:40.570
What carries that pull all the way down to the roots is a peculiarity of water itself.

74
00:07:40.570 --> 00:07:46.460
Water molecules grip one another through hydrogen bonds, and that grip is astonishingly strong.

75
00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:53.790
In the laboratory, pure water with no air bubbles in it can withstand tens of megapascals of tension before it parts.

76
00:07:53.790 --> 00:07:59.680
Under the right conditions, in other words, water behaves less like a liquid than like a wire.

77
00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:07.170
And that is exactly what the tree builds: a single unbroken thread of water running from leaf to root with no gap anywhere along it.

78
00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:16.260
A droplet evaporating at the top twitches the thread upward, and the thread, at its lowest end, draws a fresh droplet out of the soil through the root hairs.

79
00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:21.190
Galileo's rope hanging from the ceiling was right, once you turn it upside down.

80
00:08:21.190 --> 00:08:25.800
The pipes the water runs through are made of dead cells inside the wood.

81
00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:35.050
The tree builds the cells, stiffens their walls with lignin, then empties out their contents, leaving hollow cylinders stacked end to end with their ends opened.

82
00:08:35.050 --> 00:08:46.220
In conifers like pine and redwood these are narrow cells a few millimeters long and 10 to 40 micrometers wide, and water seeps from one into the next through thin membraned pits in their walls.

83
00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:52.430
In flowering trees the cell ends have dissolved away completely, producing true pipes meters in length.

84
00:08:52.430 --> 00:09:03.120
That structural difference shows up directly in the speed of flow: in a pine, water advances 1 to 2 meters an hour, while in a wide-vesseled oak it can reach 40 meters an hour.

85
00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:12.850
This explanation was put forward in the 1890s independently by Dixon, Joly and Askenasy, and for a long time the idea was thought unbelievable.

86
00:09:12.850 --> 00:09:19.380
The reason was this: if the water is being pulled, the pressure inside the pipe has to be below zero.

87
00:09:19.380 --> 00:09:21.750
A pressure lower than a vacuum.

88
00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:29.080
Measuring that took half a century, and it became possible in the 1960s with the pressure chamber technique Scholander developed.

89
00:09:29.080 --> 00:09:36.890
A leaf is cut and sealed in a chamber with its stalk protruding, and the pressure inside is raised until water appears at the cut end.

90
00:09:36.890 --> 00:09:41.660
The pressure required is the mirror image of the tension that was inside that leaf.

91
00:09:41.660 --> 00:09:44.350
The measurements confirmed the theory.

92
00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:54.080
At midday the tension at the crown of a tall tree runs between minus 1 and minus 2 megapascals, and in desert plants far more extreme values are recorded.

93
00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:56.770
That closes the energy account as well.

94
00:09:56.770 --> 00:10:08.260
The gravitational cost of carrying water 100 meters up is about 0.01 megapascals per meter, so 1 megapascal in total, with friction losses in the pipes on top of it.

95
00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:14.470
The Sun pays every bit of that bill, because the thing doing the work is evaporation itself.

96
00:10:14.470 --> 00:10:18.760
The tree does not spend a single molecule of sugar lifting water.

97
00:10:18.760 --> 00:10:31.850
Its cost lies elsewhere: to take in carbon dioxide it has to open its stomata, and through those open pores it loses between 200 and 400 molecules of water for every molecule of carbon dioxide it gains.

98
00:10:31.850 --> 00:10:34.700
That is why the tree moves water at all.

99
00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:39.310
The rising column is the invoice photosynthesis pays.

100
00:10:39.310 --> 00:10:43.120
But water under tension is in a dangerous state.

101
00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:51.730
At pressures below zero, liquid water is in what physicists call a metastable condition: on paper it should already have boiled away,

102
00:10:51.730 --> 00:10:56.980
and the only thing keeping it liquid is the refusal of its molecules to let go of one another.

103
00:10:56.980 --> 00:11:01.910
Let the smallest air bubble into a column like that and the bubble does not close up.

104
00:11:01.910 --> 00:11:13.880
The opposite happens: because the pressure outside is higher than the pressure within, the bubble expands explosively, fills the whole conduit in an instant, and puts that conduit permanently out of service.

105
00:11:13.880 --> 00:11:19.290
The process is called cavitation, and the blocked pipe is called an embolism.

106
00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:25.340
The air usually gets in from an empty neighboring conduit, sucked through the pores of the pit membrane between them.

107
00:11:25.340 --> 00:11:34.430
As drought deepens the tension rises, and as tension rises air can pass through ever smaller pores, so the blockage spreads from one pipe to its neighbors.

108
00:11:34.430 --> 00:11:36.800
This is an audible event.

109
00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:45.890
In the middle of the 1960s, researchers pressed sensitive detectors against drying trunks and managed to count the ultrasonic clicks given off as the columns snapped.

110
00:11:45.890 --> 00:11:50.340
A tree running out of water crackles, in the literal sense of the word.

111
00:11:50.340 --> 00:11:53.670
Trees have defenses against this.

112
00:11:53.670 --> 00:12:01.480
The first is sheer number: millions of conduits run in parallel in the trunk, and the loss of a few hundred does not stop the flow.

113
00:12:01.480 --> 00:12:04.010
The second is design.

114
00:12:04.010 --> 00:12:15.020
In conifers the pit membrane between cells carries a thick plug at its center; when the neighboring cell fills with air, the pressure difference shoves that plug against the mouth of the pit and stoppers the hole,

115
00:12:15.020 --> 00:12:18.190
sealing the blockage off before it can spread.

116
00:12:18.190 --> 00:12:20.720
The third is repair.

117
00:12:20.720 --> 00:12:31.250
Gas bubbles released from water that froze over winter leave much of the tree blocked come spring, and this is where root pressure genuinely earns its keep: it is used to refill the columns.

118
00:12:31.250 --> 00:12:37.140
That spring flow, the source of maple syrup, is in fact a repair operation.

119
00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:43.510
For all these defenses, a tree still has to place a bet on how close to its breaking point it is willing to run.

120
00:12:43.510 --> 00:12:50.200
Close its stomata early and it stays safe but goes hungry; close them late and it grows but blocks its pipes.

121
00:12:50.200 --> 00:12:59.770
A wide-ranging review published in 2012 pooled data on 226 forest species from 81 different regions of the world, and the result was striking:

122
00:12:59.770 --> 00:13:07.260
70 percent of the species were operating with a safety margin narrower than 1 megapascal from their blockage threshold.

123
00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:12.350
What is more, this did not distinguish wet tropical forest from arid scrub.

124
00:13:12.350 --> 00:13:18.400
Forests, wherever they grow, appear to have chosen to live right at the edge of their own limits.

125
00:13:18.400 --> 00:13:21.570
Which brings us to the ceiling on height.

126
00:13:21.570 --> 00:13:33.700
In a study led by Koch and colleagues in 2004, researchers climbed California's tallest redwoods on rope and harness, among them the tallest known at the time, a tree of 112.7 meters,

127
00:13:33.700 --> 00:13:37.350
a few meters shorter than today's record holder.

128
00:13:37.350 --> 00:13:43.880
What they measured at the top was this: even with the soil soaking wet and the roots under no strain at all,

129
00:13:43.880 --> 00:13:51.690
gravity and pipe friction alone left the tension inside the topmost needles about 1 megapascal greater than in the needles below.

130
00:13:51.690 --> 00:13:56.620
And the marks of that difference were written into the leaves themselves.

131
00:13:56.620 --> 00:14:04.270
The higher you went, the shorter and thicker the needles became, the heavier per unit area, the more tightly they pressed against the stem.

132
00:14:04.270 --> 00:14:09.360
The reason is simple: a leaf grows by its cells taking up water and swelling.

133
00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:15.410
The tension at the crown is so high that the cells cannot swell enough, and the leaf cannot expand.

134
00:14:15.410 --> 00:14:19.860
And when the leaf cannot expand, photosynthesis falls.

135
00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:27.830
When the researchers extended these height-dependent curves, they found a crossing point: the height at which leaves could no longer expand at all,

136
00:14:27.830 --> 00:14:35.640
and therefore at which the tree could not invest another centimeter upward, came out between 122 and 130 meters.

137
00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:39.610
So the tallest trees alive today have struck the ceiling.

138
00:14:39.610 --> 00:14:46.300
And what sets that ceiling is not the strength of the wood, not wind, not soil, not nutrients.

139
00:14:46.300 --> 00:14:52.190
It is whether a handful of needles at the very top can pull enough water from a stretched thread of water to swell.

140
00:14:52.190 --> 00:14:55.040
That limit carries a new meaning now.

141
00:14:55.040 --> 00:15:04.930
As the air warms, its drying power rises even at the same humidity, which means the pull bearing down on the leaf, and so the tension in the column, increases.

142
00:15:04.930 --> 00:15:11.620
For trees whose safety margins were already narrow, that amounts to a systematic drift toward the breaking threshold.

143
00:15:11.620 --> 00:15:22.310
It is what happened in the great Amazon droughts of 2005 and 2010: across wide areas, trees died not of thirst but because their conducting systems filled with air.

144
00:15:22.310 --> 00:15:28.520
Even a tree with its roots in wet soil becomes unable to reach water once the column in its pipes has parted.

145
00:15:28.520 --> 00:15:32.970
Engineering's attempts to imitate this plumbing are still at the crawling stage.

146
00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:44.460
In 2008 researchers managed to hold water at a stable tension of around minus 1 megapascal inside a tiny structure built from a gel with nanometer-scale pores, and to make it flow by evaporation:

147
00:15:44.460 --> 00:15:48.110
a working artificial tree in the laboratory.

148
00:15:48.110 --> 00:15:53.360
But that device was a few centimeters across, and it lasted hours rather than weeks.

149
00:15:53.360 --> 00:15:59.890
Nobody has yet built a version 100 meters tall that repairs itself and keeps running for 2,000 years.

150
00:15:59.890 --> 00:16:10.420
The forest does it every summer, quietly, without a single moving part, on nothing but the drying power of the Sun and the stubbornness of water molecules that will not let go of one another.

151
00:16:10.420 --> 00:16:14.390
Nöron Science'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

152
00:16:14.390 --> 00:16:17.720
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
